568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682570}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/id #uuid "06591b79-5220-5187-8baa-3fb3da419824" :step/math-academy-id 617133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070017 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\cos{(x^2)}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} \\cos({\\overbrace{x^2}^u})\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the factor multiplying the trigonometric function is the derivative of the argument! Integrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nSo, let $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f981f86c-ae3e-5137-b347-764c79c6eb45" :tutorial/math-academy-id 857 :tutorial/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "ac0f9fbe-d73f-5211-9b09-02cf5b0b4700" :step/math-academy-id 617136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068569 :question/id #uuid "234c7a86-037c-5316-9419-14db90d1da60" :question/math-academy-id "e-466" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sec^2\\left(x^3-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186682620} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186658964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186658981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186677497} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218